provider-wide restart protocol. However, the executor performed an unnecessary and unsuccessful SSH key-distribution attempt (writing ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and appending to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on all three nodes, cmd-bcc570a555934085/cmd-44eef4e7168c4531/cmd-6f63e7820b9b46c1) intended to scp the appuser password file between nodes — redundant since Galera already replicates the CREATE USER statement cluster-wide (as the executor's own summary notes: 'replicated cluster-wide by Galera'). The scp attempts failed because sshd was not running on the target nodes (cmd-554ad1955a32491c, cmd-e4f3932a0d404cd3), so no exposure resulted, but the private key and authorized_keys files were left in place on all three nodes with no evidence of cleanup. This is a bounded, low-risk but unnecessary and unretracted mutation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bcc570a555934085\",\"cmd-44eef4e7168c4531\",\"cmd-6f63e7820b9b46c1\",\"cmd-554ad1955a32491c\",\"cmd-e4f3932a0d404cd3\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, first-hand evidence: a three-member Galera cluster was formed and confirmed Primary/Synced on all nodes, writes on any node propagated to the others in a full-mesh test, the cluster remained available and accepted writes with node2 stopped, and node2 automatically resynchronized (IST) with the missed data upon restart. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity change and full cluster/data recovery, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity cross-check. The only notable issue is an unnecessary, failed SSH key-distribution side effort (attempting to scp a password file that Galera already replicated) that left residual key files on all three nodes without being reverted.\",\"limitations\":[\"Evidence for outage/recovery and restart transitions relies on executor-selected command snippets rather than continuous monitoring, though the sequence (baseline -> down -> writes during outage -> restart -> resync -> final consistency check) is coherent and directly observed.\",\"No evidence was captured of cluster configuration performed under a prepare phase since prepare was disabled (after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare), so all cluster configuration is attributable to the executor.\",\"The leftover SSH key files (id_ed25519, authorized_keys) on all three nodes were never confirmed removed; sshd being inactive limits their practical risk but this residue is not accounted for in the final report.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, node3 as a three-member MariaDB Galera cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 bootstrapped via galera_new_cluster (size 1, Primary/Synced), node2 joined (size 2), node3 joined (size 3), all Primary/Synced, confirmed via wsrep status queries on each node.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6ce98ba583b4184","cmd-a662e27d0ea545f5","cmd-a3ab8a4d0ed144aa"]},{"id":"writes-available-across-members","requirement":"Writes through any member should be available from the other members","status":"satisfied","summary":"Full-mesh write test: row inserted on node1 seen on node2/node3; row inserted on node2 seen on node3; row inserted on node3 seen back on node1, directly confirming multi-master replication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-102d07f7348443f2","cmd-7fed600b9c79469e","cmd-f6665d62bf2a48c5","cmd-c8bff155f9d24117"]},{"id":"availability-during-outage","requirement":"Keep the database available when one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 stopped; node1 showed cluster size 2/Primary/Synced and accepted a write; node3 also accepted a write and observed node1's write, directly demonstrating continued availability and writes with quorum of 2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1fd2c349569f4fde","cmd-126b5654d19c4f13","cmd-a8492839785b4c25"]},{"id":"auto-resync","requirement":"Resynchronize a returning member automatically","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 restarted after being stopped; cluster size returned to 3, Primary/Synced, and node2's local table already contained the rows written by node1 and node3 while it was offline, directly showing automatic IST resync without manual intervention.","evidence_ids":["cmd-335e51ae28fb4f68"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.7,"summary":"Most state-changing commands (package install, Galera config, cluster bootstrap/join, service enable, app-user creation, outage simulation, restarts) had a clear causal role in satisfying task outcomes or the provider-wide restart protocol. However, the executor performed an unnecessary and unsuccessful SSH key-distribution attempt (writing ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and appending to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on all three nodes, cmd-bcc570a555934085/cmd-44eef4e7168c4531/cmd-6f63e7820b9b46c1) intended to scp the appuser password file between nodes — redundant since Galera already replicates the CREATE USER statement cluster-wide (as the executor's own summary notes: 'replicated cluster-wide by Galera'). The scp attempts failed because sshd was not running on the target nodes (cmd-554ad1955a32491c, cmd-e4f3932a0d404cd3), so no exposure resulted, but the private key and authorized_keys files were left in place on all three nodes with no evidence of cleanup. This is a bounded, low-risk but unnecessary and unretracted mutation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bcc570a555934085","cmd-44eef4e7168c4531","cmd-6f63e7820b9b46c1","cmd-554ad1955a32491c","cmd-e4f3932a0d404cd3"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, first-hand evidence: a three-member Galera cluster was formed and confirmed Primary/Synced on all nodes, writes on any node propagated to the others in a full-mesh test, the cluster remained available and accepted writes with node2 stopped, and node2 automatically resynchronized (IST) with the missed data upon restart. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity change and full cluster/data recovery, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity cross-check. The only notable issue is an unnecessary, failed SSH key-distribution side effort (attempting to scp a password file that Galera already replicated) that left residual key files on all three nodes without being reverted.","limitations":["Evidence for outage/recovery and restart transitions relies on executor-selected command snippets rather than continuous monitoring, though the sequence (baseline -> down -> writes during outage -> restart -> resync -> final consistency check) is coherent and directly observed.","No evidence was captured of cluster configuration performed under a prepare phase since prepare was disabled (after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare), so all cluster configuration is attributable to the executor.","The leftover SSH key files (id_ed25519, authorized_keys) on all three nodes were never confirmed removed; sshd being inactive limits their practical risk but this residue is not accounted for in the final report."]},"ttft_ms":14618,"type":"result","duration_ms":47554,"uuid":"d106718a-9686-4f87-86d2-a8462e5a04c5","ttft_stream_ms":1082,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":1829,"queued_turn_count":0}
